// @check
// @error no `n` on `T` — its bound `i32` is not a type with members
// @error `i32` is not a bound — a bound is a type with method members (`type i32 = { m(): R }`) or an inline `{ m(): R }`
// THE WHOLE REMAINING DOMAIN OF `tpMemberFloorMsg`, and this file is what says so. An
// UNBOUNDED `<T>` is an entirely open hole and never reaches that rung (D1431); a
// WELL-FORMED bound is answered by `boundMemberFloorMsg`, which names the bound and what
// it grants. What is left is a bound whose annotation names no type with members.
//
// TWO DIAGNOSTICS, AND THEY DIVIDE THE WORK. The member sentence says which read failed
// and stops. The bound's own sentence, at the instantiation, says what is wrong with the
// declaration and how to spell one. The member sentence must not repeat that, and must
// not tell an author to declare the bound they already declared — which is what it did
// until the 2026-09-03 ruling made the unbounded reading of it wrong at every receiver
// that can still reach it.
function f<T: i32>(x: T): i32 {
  x.n
}

print(f(1))
